<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Siege</p><h1 class="enc-title">Siege of Vicksburg</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1863">1863</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-civil-war">American Civil War</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Siege of Vicksburg, from May to 4 July 1863, ended with the surrender of the Confederate fortress on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-river">Mississippi River</a> in Mississippi to Union forces under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulysses-s-grant">Ulysses S. Grant</a>. Its fall gave the Union control of the Mississippi and split the Confederacy in two. Coming a day after Gettysburg, it marked a turning point of the war.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/siege-of-vicksburg.jpg" alt="Siege of Vicksburg" width="320" height="235" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Thure de Thulstrup · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Thure_de_Thulstrup_-_Siege_of_Vicksburg_-_Assault_on_Fort_Hill.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The siege followed manoeuvres in which Major General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulysses-s-grant">Ulysses S. Grant</a> and his Army of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tennessee">Tennessee</a> crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-river">Mississippi River</a> at Bruinsburg, won battles at Port Gibson and Raymond and captured Jackson, the state capital, on 14 May 1863. Lieutenant General John C. Pemberton's Confederate Army of Mississippi failed to stop the advance at Champion Hill and Big Black River Bridge and withdrew into the fortress city of Vicksburg, the last major Confederate stronghold on the river.</p><p>Pemberton could put only 18,000 troops into his lines against more than 35,000 for Grant, but the line, about six and a half miles long, ran over steep hills studded with forts, redoubts and gun pits. General Joseph E. Johnston, whom many in the city expected to relieve it, never did.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Grant's assault on the Stockade Redan on 19 May was repulsed, at a cost of 157 killed, 777 wounded and eight missing against Confederate losses of eight killed and 62 wounded. A larger assault on 22 May was also repulsed. On 25 May Grant ordered the reduction of the city by regular approaches, to avoid further loss of life, and Union trenches closed round the Confederates, trapped between them and the gunboats on the river. On 25 June a mine packed with 2,200 pounds of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gunpowder">gunpowder</a> blew a crater under the 3rd Louisiana Redan, but the infantry that followed was pinned down and fell back.</p><p>On 3 July Pemberton asked about negotiations. Grant first demanded unconditional surrender, then offered to parole the prisoners, as he did not want to feed 30,000 Confederates in prison camps. Pemberton surrendered on 4 July.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>With the surrender of Port Hudson to Major General Nathaniel P. Banks on 9 July, the Union gained command of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-river">Mississippi River</a> and held it for the rest of the war. The surrender cut the Trans-Mississippi Department off from the rest of the Confederacy, effectively splitting it in two. It is sometimes considered, together with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-e-lee">Robert E. Lee</a>'s defeat at Gettysburg the day before, the turning point of the war, and Lincoln called Vicksburg the key to the war. The works around the city are kept by the National Park Service as Vicksburg National Military Park.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Siege of Vicksburg” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
